, {'source_file': 'src/test/java/org/quantumbadger/redreader/common/GeneralKeyMaterialTest.kt', 'symbol': 'GeneralKeyMaterialTest', 'edit_kind': 'create', 'original_code': '', 'corrected_code': 'package org.quantumbadger.redreader.common\n\nimport javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec\nimport org.junit.Assert.assertArrayEquals\nimport org.junit.Test\n\nclass GeneralKeyMaterialTest {\n\n    @Test\n    fun encodedBytesArePreservedAndDefensivelyCopiedForAesKeyConstruction() {\n        val originalBytes = byteArrayOf(\n            0x01, 0x23, 0x45, 0x67,\n            0x10, 0x32, 0x54, 0x76,\n            0x11, 0x22, 0x33, 0x44,\n            0x55, 0x66, 0x77, 0x7f\n        )\n        val keyMaterial = General.AesKeyMaterial(originalBytes)\n\n        val firstEncoded = keyMaterial.getEncoded()\n        assertArrayEquals(originalBytes, firstEncoded)\n\n        firstEncoded[0] = 0\n        val encodedForAes = keyMaterial.getEncoded()\n        assertArrayEquals(originalBytes, encodedForAes)\n\n        val aesKey = SecretKeySpec(encodedForAes, "AES")\n        assertArrayEquals(originalBytes, aesKey.encoded)\n    }\n}\n', 'reason': 'Adds the required JUnit regression coverage for General.AesKeyMaterial without reflection: encoded key bytes equal the input, a mutated returned array cannot affect a later encoded result, and the bytes passed into AES SecretKeySpec remain unchanged.'}], 'changed_files': ['src/main/java/org/quantumbadger/redreader/common/General.kt', 'src/test/java/org/quantumbadger/redreader/common/GeneralKeyMaterialTest.kt'], 'quality_score': 100, 'explanation': '<!-- pr-title: Route AES key bytes through SecretKey encoding -->\nI found that `parseConfig` decrypts configuration data by constructing an AES `SecretKeySpec` directly from `b1`. During runtime analysis, the tool did not observe those bytes being prepared through `Key.getEncoded()` before that construction.\n\nI added `AesKeyMaterial` to store a defensive copy in an AES `SecretKey` and pass its encoded bytes 